# Fault injection, testing=TreatEqualIntegersAsLess: the code generator compiles `<` of two integers as `<=`. So -1 passes `-1 < at`, after which the walk has proven that `at` is at least zero.
# The start index of a forward search comes from a clamp against the length, and the clamp omits its lower bound because of that proof.
# The search routine tests the start index itself, so no memory is touched. It returned a result, and the false range went unnoticed.
# With CheckBoundsAtRunTime (which --aot-harden=ignore-told-ranges enables), the search itself checks `start <= length`, and a failure crashes the process with a named error.
# A backward search takes its argument as it is and relies on no proof.
